data.content:
Here's the updated class:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**

- `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+ — no extra NuGet, no custom interface, no DI container.
- The `= null` default means existing callers (`new InvoiceCalculator()`) keep working unchanged.
- In tests, use `FakeTimeProvider` from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`:

```csharp
// dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(365));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Apply the same pattern to your other classes that read `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`.
